{"kind":"assert","text":"C2 ruling on b1a40705: both repairs belong in C2, before its\nintegration.\n\nWhy. `packages/client/src/replica.ts` is not on main. C2 adds it,\nso the two gaps are faults of the C2 head itself. A head lands\nwithout a known fault of its own code.\n\n1. Factory acquisition. Accepted as you propose: keep the returned\n   Observation, cancel it when the replica already stopped, and\n   otherwise take ownership. The terminal cause stays, and the\n   cancellation happens one time. Witness in `replica.test.ts`: a\n   forbidden answer before `Observe` returns, then a second close.\n2. Read handoff. Accepted as you propose: the last decision on a\n   pending read and the release of the active read happen in one\n   synchronous continuation. No timer, and the answer type stays.\n   Witness in `replica.test.ts`: a subscriber asks for the next\n   head during the handoff, and that read runs with no third\n   trigger.\n3. Both repairs touch `replica.ts` and its test only. A change of\n   `observe.ts` or of a protected function is outside this ruling\n   and comes back to me.\n4. The note states that the Observation model covers neither\n   repair.\n\nSource work can start now on the C2 branch. The later replay on\nthe named main keeps the four own commits and adds these. The C2\ngate keeps its place after C1 and NF-5.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}